ChatSched — Claude fixes, Sept 3
=================================

Scope: this file documents ONLY the third task resolved from
"Claude To fix 1..txt" — item 7, CRITICAL: Payout RPCs are insufficiently
protected. Items 5 and 6 were already completed and documented separately
("claude fixes Sept 1" and "claude fixes Sept 2"). Items 8 onward were
left untouched.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Important context found while investigating, before anything else
------------------------------------------------------------------------
workers/README.md is explicit: this whole payout/ledger pipeline is
"experimental, not-production-ready... nothing here runs unless you
deliberately start these processes yourself." The live payout mechanism
today is the "Mark payout sent" button in Admin.tsx's Requests tab,
completely separate from any of this. I'm recording this for accuracy —
it does NOT reduce the severity of the fix. Every one of these tables and
functions is already deployed and reachable over the live Supabase API
the moment these migrations run, regardless of whether anyone ever starts
the Node workers. An unauthenticated or ordinary logged-in user hitting
this API today is a real exposure independent of whether the batch/ledger
pipeline is "finished."

------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. What the finding said, verified against the real source
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two separate, both-real problems, confirmed by reading the actual SQL:

  A) schema_payouts_phase1.sql never calls `alter table ... enable row
     level security` for any of its four new tables (publisher_ledger,
     payouts, payout_items, payout_provider_events) — every other table
     in this schema does this immediately after creation. Combined with
     Supabase's default project-level grants (every public-schema table
     gets full CRUD access for `anon`/`authenticated` unless RLS says
     otherwise), this meant, before this fix, ANY authenticated user —
     not just admins — could directly SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE these
     tables via PostgREST: a publisher could INSERT a positive
     publisher_ledger row crediting themselves, or UPDATE payouts.status
     straight to 'completed', completely bypassing every RPC.

  B) create_payout_batch(), mark_payout_item_attempt(), and
     update_payout_status() (schema_payouts_functions.sql) never call
     is_admin() at all. approve_payout() already does — the finding's
     own text confirms this one is fine. Because these are `security
     definer` functions, fixing (A) alone would NOT have protected them
     — a security definer function runs with the function owner's
     privileges regardless of RLS on the tables it touches, so the only
     real gate for these three has to be an explicit in-function check.

  Plus the specific idempotency gap named in the finding:
  mark_payout_item_attempt() inserted a negative publisher_ledger row
  every time it received p_status = 'succeeded', with no check for
  whether that ledger entry already existed — confirmed by reading the
  function body directly.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. What I found while tracing this, beyond the finding's own description
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Traced every real caller of all four functions before deciding how to
gate each one:

  - src/pages/AdminPayouts.tsx calls create_payout_batch() and
    approve_payout() via supabase.rpc() — a real logged-in admin session
    (it's a tab inside /admin, already gated by
    <RequireAuth role="admin"> in App.tsx). auth.uid() is set here.

  - workers/payoutWorker.js calls mark_payout_item_attempt() over a raw
    `pg` connection using DATABASE_URL — a direct Postgres connection,
    not a PostgREST/Supabase-client session. auth.uid() is null there.
    workers/webhookServer.js writes to payout_items and
    publisher_ledger the same way (raw SQL, same DATABASE_URL pattern) —
    it doesn't call mark_payout_item_attempt() at all, it duplicates the
    logic inline. That duplication is a real, separate observation
    (see "what's not done" below) but out of this task's scope, since
    the finding's own affected-files list names only the two SQL files.

  - update_payout_status() has no caller anywhere in this codebase today
    (its own comment calls it "Optional") — still live and callable by
    anyone before this fix.

  This confirms a bare `is_admin()` check on mark_payout_item_attempt()
  and update_payout_status() would have been WRONG — it would have
  rejected the trusted worker's own null-auth.uid() context along with
  genuine attackers, breaking real (if experimental) functionality. This
  codebase already has an established idiom for exactly this
  distinction — prevent_self_verification() / prevent_role_change() both
  use "auth.uid() is not null and not is_admin()" to mean "block a real
  logged-in non-admin session, but don't block a trusted server-side
  context where there's no session at all." I used the same shape here
  for the three functions that needed a new check, and left
  approve_payout()'s existing bare is_admin() check untouched — that one
  is meant to require an actual human admin decision even from a trusted
  connection, and the finding says it's already correct.

  I also traced webhookServer.js's own raw SQL and found it already
  implements exactly the idempotency guard the finding says
  mark_payout_item_attempt() is missing (a "not exists" check against
  publisher_ledger keyed on reference_id + type = 'payout') — I used the
  identical pattern inside the RPC itself, since that's the literal gap
  named.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Files changed
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- supabase/schema_phase83_secure_payout_rpcs.sql (NEW)
    - Enables RLS on all four payout tables. publisher_ledger, payouts,
      and payout_items each get a single admin-only policy (`for all
      using (is_admin()) with check (is_admin())`) — confirmed via a
      full grep of src/ that AdminPayouts.tsx is the only frontend
      consumer of any of these tables today, so admin-only matches
      actual current need exactly. payout_provider_events gets an
      admin-only SELECT policy only (no insert/update/delete policy at
      all for any authenticated-role caller) — it's written exclusively
      by webhookServer.js's own raw DATABASE_URL connection, which
      bypasses RLS entirely regardless, so there's no legitimate
      PostgREST-level writer to allow for.
    - Revokes EXECUTE on all four RPCs from PUBLIC, then grants it back
      to `authenticated` only — so `anon` (a logged-out visitor) can no
      longer even attempt any of these calls, regardless of what the
      in-function check would have decided. Defense in depth alongside,
      not instead of, the checks below.
    - create_payout_batch(): adds the auth.uid()-aware admin/worker
      check, and adds `perform pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext(
      'create_payout_batch'))` at the top of the transaction to
      serialize concurrent batch-generation calls — the finding's "lock
      the relevant ledger/balance rows during batch generation."
      publisher_available_balance is an aggregate view with no single
      row to `for update` lock per publisher, so an advisory lock
      serializing the whole (infrequent, admin-triggered) operation is
      the correct tool here rather than a row lock that doesn't apply to
      an aggregate read. Every other line of the function body is
      unchanged from schema_payouts_functions.sql.
    - mark_payout_item_attempt(): adds the same admin/worker check; adds
      an explicit allowed-transition whitelist (modelled on the actual
      transitions payoutWorker.js and webhookServer.js use today —
      pending/processing -> sent/failed — plus the pre-existing
      succeeded branch, with succeeded and failed both terminal, no
      transition backward ever allowed); and makes the ledger insert on
      succeeded idempotent via the same "no existing payout ledger row
      for this item" guard webhookServer.js's own raw SQL already uses.
    - update_payout_status(): adds the same admin/worker check and its
      own explicit allowed-transition whitelist for the payouts.status
      lifecycle (pending/approved/processing/file_generated/sent, with
      completed/failed/canceled terminal). Deliberately excludes
      'approved' from every allowed target — that transition stays
      exclusively approve_payout()'s job, so this more general-purpose
      function can't be used to route around its dedicated pending-only
      check.

- supabase/tests/payout_rpc_authorization_test.sql (NEW)
    - pgTAP test file, 15 assertions. Proves: RLS blocks a non-admin from
      seeing any row in all four tables, while an admin sees them fully;
      all three previously-unprotected RPCs return ok:false for a
      non-admin; an anonymous session gets a real Postgres permission
      error (not a jsonb response) attempting any of them, proving the
      PUBLIC execute revoke actually took effect; an admin can walk a
      real payout item through pending -> sent -> succeeded with exactly
      one ledger entry created; an admin attempting to move that same
      item backward out of succeeded is rejected as an invalid
      transition; and an admin's create_payout_batch() call succeeds
      end-to-end, correctly picking up a publisher's real positive
      ledger balance into a new batch.

- supabase/tests/README.md (EDITED)
    - Added a section documenting how to run
      payout_rpc_authorization_test.sql and what it covers.

- Claude To fix 1..txt (EDITED)
    - Marked item 7 as [COMPLETED], pointing at the new migration, the
      new test file, and this document.

