5. Why this shape, not a different one
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Admin-only RLS on all four tables, not a scoped "publisher can see
  their own payout_items/ledger rows" policy, because no current
  frontend page needs that (EarningsDashboard.tsx computes a publisher's
  own figures from publishers/requests directly, not from this ledger) —
  adding read access nobody asked for would widen the surface again for
  no benefit. A future feature that wants publishers to see their own
  payout history can add that policy deliberately, as its own decision.
- The "auth.uid() is not null and not is_admin()" check, not a bare
  is_admin(), on the three previously-unprotected functions, because a
  bare check would have broken the actual trusted worker path that calls
  mark_payout_item_attempt() today (confirmed by reading
  payoutWorker.js's connection code directly, not assumed) — and this
  codebase already has an established, correct idiom for exactly this
  distinction elsewhere.
- Left approve_payout() completely untouched beyond the PUBLIC-execute
  revoke, because the finding's own text says it's already correct, and
  because releasing real money is arguably the single most sensitive
  action in this whole pipeline — requiring a real human admin session
  even from an otherwise-trusted connection is a deliberate, reasonable
  design choice worth preserving exactly as it was written, not
  "fixing" it to match the other three.
- An advisory transaction lock for the batch-generation race, not a row
  lock, because publisher_available_balance is an aggregate view (SUMs
  across two tables) with no single row that represents "this
  publisher's balance" to lock — the standard, correct Postgres tool for
  serializing an infrequent whole-operation race like this is
  pg_advisory_xact_lock, not a workaround like locking arbitrary
  ledger rows that wouldn't actually prevent the race.

