6. What's NOT done, and why that's a deliberate boundary here
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Items 8 through 13 in "Claude To fix 1..txt" are untouched.
- A real, separate design smell I noticed while tracing this (not part
  of the finding, not fixed here): webhookServer.js duplicates
  mark_payout_item_attempt()'s ledger-insert logic inline via raw SQL
  instead of calling the RPC, so the "succeeded" path now has two
  independent implementations of the same idempotency guard that have to
  be kept in sync by hand. Worth unifying at some point, but the
  finding's own affected-files list names only the two SQL files, not
  the Node workers, so I left workers/webhookServer.js and
  workers/payoutWorker.js completely untouched.
- Claude to fix 2.txt and Claude to fix 3.txt were not opened.
- Same standing honesty note this project's test suite has carried since
  it began: no Postgres, Docker, or Supabase CLI in this sandbox, so
  neither the new migration nor the new test file has been run against a
  real database. Everything here was reviewed by hand, line-by-line,
  against the actual current definitions of every function and every
  real caller in the codebase — not written from memory of what they
  "should" say. The first real `supabase test db` run is the actual test
  of whether this is correct.
